sections.content:
The three concepts, verified:

```js
// HOISTING: var is usable as undefined before its line
console.log(hoisted); // undefined (var was hoisted + initialized to undefined)
var hoisted = 42;

// TDZ: let/const are known but NOT usable before their line
try {
  console.log(tdz);   // ReferenceError: Cannot access 'tdz' before initialization
} catch(e) { console.log(e.message) }
let tdz = 'now fine';

// SCOPE: var is function-scoped; let/const are block-scoped
function demo() {
  if (true) {
    var fnScoped = 1;   // visible in ALL of demo()
    let blockScoped = 2; // visible only in this block
  }
  console.log(fnScoped);    // 1 (leaked from the block)
  // console.log(blockScoped); // ReferenceError — block is gone
}
demo();
```

Hoisting, precisely: the engine processes declarations during compilation, BEFORE running the code. var declarations are hoisted and INITIALIZED to undefined (the variable exists as undefined from the function's start). let and const are hoisted but NOT initialized — they're in the TDZ from the block's start to the declaration line — accessing them throws ReferenceError.

The practical rules:
1. Using a var before its line: undefined (silent bug — usually a typo in a different order)
2. Using a let/const before its line: ReferenceError (the loud error — the design improvement)
3. Function declarations are fully hoisted (the body is available before the declaration line)
4. Function expressions are NOT hoisted (the variable follows its var/let rules)

The TDZ's purpose: to prevent the exact class of bug var enabled — accessing a variable before its declaration. By making it an error instead of undefined, the bug surfaces at development time, not as a NaN propagating through three functions.
